    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/primera-junta.jpg" alt="Primera Junta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta">Primera Junta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Primera_junta_por_Francisco_Fortuny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco Fortuny, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Provinces grew out of the collapse of Spanish authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s capture of Ferdinand VII left the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> under Baltasar Hidalgo de Cisneros. The criollos, legally Spanish but excluded from the great offices and moved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and liberal ideas, demanded his dismissal under the principle that sovereignty returned to the people when the king was absent. On 24 or 25 May 1810 an Open Cabildo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> removed him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta">Primera Junta</a> was formed on 25 May under Cornelio Saavedra, with Mariano Moreno and Juan José Paso as secretaries.</p><p>The Junta swore loyalty to Ferdinand VII and rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>'s puppet government, but refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-regency-of-spain-and-the-indies">Council of Regency of Spain and the Indies</a>, in which no Spanish American had taken part, and sent circulars asking the cities of the former viceroyalty for deputies. Moreno and his friends, inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> by their opponents, drove its policies; Saavedra's followers were more moderate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>'s counter-revolution under the former viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers">Santiago de Liniers</a> was crushed and he was shot, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> swore to the Regency and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before any independence was declared.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/assembly-of-the-year-xiii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assembly-of-the-year-xiii.jpg" alt="Assembly of the Year XIII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/assembly-of-the-year-xiii">Assembly of the Year XIII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asamblea_del_a%C3%B1o_XIII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The revolution divided society by interest as well as idea. Against it stood the ecclesiastical elite, the viceregal bureaucracy and the rich merchants of the Spanish commercial monopoly, entrenched in the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> and the Cabildo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. For it were a petty bourgeoisie of lawyers such as Moreno, Castelli, Belgrano and Paso, popular priests, doctors and common workers like Domingo French and Antonio Beruti, and a new merchant bourgeoisie of criollo and British houses whose trade with other countries, long treated as smuggling, had been legalised in 1809 in the crisis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s fall. Moreno kept tariffs on imports despite British pressure, which set the merchants against him.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta">Primera Junta</a> set up a fund for mining, redistributed land in the Pampas, limited the concentration of great estates, and launched enterprises to make rifles in Buenos Aires and Tucumán and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assembly-of-the-year-xiii">Assembly of the Year XIII</a> reshaped society by law: the Law of Wombs made the children of slaves free, any slave entering the territory was freed, the mit'a, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and yanaconazgo were abolished and Indigenous people declared citizens with equal rights, noble ranks disappeared, and the wealthy paid 500 pesos a year to finance the state and army. The Pampas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranquel">Ranquel</a> and Puelche country, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-chaco">Gran Chaco</a> Guaycuru.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin.jpg" alt="José de San Martín" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_de_San_Mart%C3%ADn_(retrato,_c.1828).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The exact author is disputed. Some sources attribute it to Jean Baptiste Madou, others to the art teacher of San Martin's …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The war began badly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> campaign under Belgrano was repelled, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>'s fleet destroyed the Junta's first squadron at the Battle of San Nicolás, and the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> campaign ended on 20 June 1811 in the Disaster of Huaqui. Belgrano held Tucumán in 1812 and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, but lost at Vilcapugio and Ayohuma; Rondeau's Third campaign lost Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> for good at Viluma on 29 November 1815. In the Eastern Bank the Oriental Revolution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a> besieged Montevideo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>, arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1812, formed the Mounted Grenadiers and won their first fight at San Lorenzo on 3 February 1813.</p><p>William Brown's squadron destroyed Montevideo's fleet at Buceo on 17 May 1814 and the city surrendered on 20 June. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a>, where he governed from 1814, San Martín built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>, crossed the Andes in six columns in January 1817, won Chacabuco on 12 February and Maipú on 5 April 1818, securing Chile. He refused to turn that army against fellow citizens. The civil war against Artigas's Federal League opened at El Espinillo on 22 February 1814; Guayabos on 10 January 1815 gave the League the whole Oriental Province, and five Directorial campaigns against Santa Fe and Entre Ríos failed. The Army of the North refused the civil war at Arequito, and at Cepeda on 1 February 1820 López and Ramírez surrounded and routed Rondeau's 2,000 men.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman.jpg" alt="Congress of Tucumán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_de_Tucum%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Angel Paganelli (1832-1928), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Government changed hands constantly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta">Primera Junta</a> gave way on 18 December 1810 to the Junta Grande, which admitted the provincial deputies and sidelined Moreno, sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and dead at sea; after the defeats of 1811 the Cabildo imposed the First Triumvirate on 23 September 1811, which issued the 1811 Provisional Statute with freedom of the press. San Martín's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lautaro">Lautaro</a> Lodge and the Patriotic Society overthrew it on 8 October 1812 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a>, which convened the General Constituent and Sovereign Assembly of 1813. The Assembly created the Directory on 31 January 1814, a Supreme Director for two years, and chose Posadas first. Alvear's 95 days in 1815 brought spies, executions without trial and a death penalty for critics until the Mutiny of Fontezuelas drove him into exile.</p><p>A Junta of Observation and the 1815 Provisional Statute followed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a>, opened on 24 March 1816, elected Pueyrredón Supreme Director on 3 May and declared independence on 9 July; Belgrano's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Plan for an Indigenous monarch was shelved. Its Constitution of 1819, sanctioned on 22 April, vested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the nation alone and leaned to monarchy; the provinces rejected it, Pueyrredón resigned on 9 June and Rondeau's Directory ended with the Congress's dissolution on 11 February 1820. Artigas's League of the Free Peoples, with its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-oriente">Congress of Oriente</a> in 1815, had offered the federal alternative throughout.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war.jpg" alt="Cisplatine War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cisplatine-War-collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See each file for details, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Provinces carried the Spanish American revolution across half a continent. Their armies freed Chile at Chacabuco and Maipú; their northern campaigns, though they lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, Jujuy and Tucumán for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The Declaration of 9 July 1816, circulated in Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a>, gave the country its independence day, and the Assembly of 1813 its anthem, the Marcha patriótica, its coat of arms, the motto En unión y libertad and the cockade and flag Belgrano had made from light blue and white. The name United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> remains an official name of Argentina under Article 35 of its constitution.</p><p>Its failures shaped its neighbours. The treaty of October 1811 recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>'s independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>; Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, lost at Viluma, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>; and the Oriental Province, abandoned to the Luso-Brazilian invasion of 1816 that Manuel García had encouraged to end the "federal disease", passed to Portugal in 1820 and emerged as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> in 1828 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a> and British intervention, by a Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> that asked no Oriental's opinion and left the borders undrawn, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s later gain. The quarrel between Buenos Aires's unitary governments and Artigas's federalism began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentine-civil-wars">Argentine Civil Wars</a> and set the terms of Argentine politics for decades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz.jpg" alt="Bernabé Aráoz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz">Bernabé Aráoz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Bernabe_Araoz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Honorio Mossi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The national government destroyed itself in the civil war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>'s Directories chose to fight the federalists rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>: Alvear offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1815, Pueyrredón let the Luso-Brazilians take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1817, and Rondeau asked them to chase "the common enemy" into Entre Ríos. The Constitution of 1819 completed the breach. Tucumán's garrison overthrew its governor on 11 November 1819 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz">Bernabé Aráoz</a> broke with the Directory; the Army of the North revolted at Arequito and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> proclaimed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>; San Martín would not bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> home.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estanislao-lopez">Estanislao López</a> marching to "oust the despots", Rondeau took 2,000 men to Cepeda and was routed on 1 February 1820. The federalists would accept no peace that left a national government standing, and on 11 February 1820 the Congress dissolved and Rondeau resigned, ending the national government of the United Provinces. The provinces governed themselves through the Anarchy of the Year XX. The constitution of 1826 adopted the name Argentine Republic and a first presidency, but it fell into disuse in 1827 amid the war with Brazil over the Oriental Province, which the Treaty of Montevideo made independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-pact">Federal Pact</a> of 1831 bound the provinces as the Argentine Confederation; fourteen of them later formed the Argentine Republic, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, Uruguay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> went their own ways.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, formed by the May Revolution of 1810, declared independence at Tucumán on 9 July 1816 and freed Chile under San Martín, but lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> and the Eastern Bank and fell to its own federalists at Cepeda in 1820; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-pact">Federal Pact</a> of 1831 made it the Argentine Confederation.</em></p>

